As I was Saying 
Pages of Past
ANDY COHEN is in much better physical shape than his latest scrapbook. El Paso’s baseball perennial, as hardy and full of vigor as ever, is getting ready to send forth on the field of battle the Texas Western diamond team.
Getting back to his newest scrapbook: The other day a man by the name of Herzel Hoxie, a resident of Newark, N.J., came to El Paso on a stopover on the way to West Coast. He’s a prosperous merchant now,)
a Newark haberdasher.
Many years ago when Andy was playing with the Newark Bears of the International League, Hoxie was the team’s batboy. He and Andy took likings for each other. So much so that Hoxie was called “Little Andy’ Cohen” by the players and fans.
Unbeknown to Andy, Hoxie started compiling a scrapbook of his sports hero. He pasted up newspaper clippings of Andy’s exploits before and after the El Pasoan played at Newark. He kept it up throughout!
Andy’s playing career.
He presented this scrapbook to Cohen on his stopover here the other day. The pages are yellowed with age and so brittle and fragile that corners break off into your hands as you turn them.
Andy's Big Day
BUT THE headlines of pages of newspaper clippings are still readable and so are the contents of the hundreds of sports stories on the El Pasoan.
It’s nearly ancient history—all of Andy’s baseball exploits. And Andy didn’t think I’d be interested in the book since I had written about his heroics many times
and had seen virtually everything written by writers who were there. 
Andy is author, or at least the co-author, of that phrase, “There is nothing deader than yesterday’s headlines.” 
One writer's application, to wit, that a hero is no hero to his valet, has a bearing on Andy and his family life. His wife and children may look on him with devotion but his past achievements in baseball take a backseat to his breadwinning proclivities. Maybe in the future some of his children will look back with pride on how “the old man” broke into the big leagues on that cold, blowy April day in 1928.
It was the opening day of the season. There was a crowd of more than 30,000 at Polo Grounds in New York City. Mayor Jimmy Walker threw out the first ball.
Overnight Fame
ANDY WAS breaking into the Giants’ lineup at second base—the position occupied the season before by the great Rogers Hornsby.
The Giants were playing the Boston Braves. Hornsby had been traded by the Giants to Boston during the winter. Before the game, Hornsby was gracious enough
to look up Andy, give him some advice and wish him luck.
In that opening game, Andy jumped more or less from obscurity to overnight fame. He was the batting hero,getting two singles and a double in helping the Giants beat the Braves 5-2. Hornsby was held to one single.)
There was bedlam and almost mayhem after the game. Fans carried Andy on their shoulders. He was pounded and pummeled. Police had to rescue him to save him from bodily injury. |

The Giants had a successor to Hornsby. John McGraw had at least found a Jewish star to appeal to the million or more Gotham people of his faith. There was leven talk that the Giants had a rival attraction to Babe Ruth, then the fabulous Yankee star. Biographies of ‘Andy sprung into print in all New York dailies. He was wined and dined and between banquet bites gave outendorsements. Anyone reading or re-reading about that 1928 opener couldn’t help but be stirred. It was THE DAY THAT WAS for Andy.


