Editor's Corner —:— 2" R. W. Lee

Ever since the good citizens of Austin proclaimed Mr. Chuck Tanner, manager of the Sun Kings, as ‘‘best actor in the Texas League for 1965” I have been axious to see the young maestro at work.

True I had seen him in earlier games this year, but that was before he took lessons in signalling from Orlando Barera, conductor of El Paso’s Symphony Orchestra.
So this past Saturday night my bride and I hied ourselves out to Dudley Field to see Mr. Tanner in action. By chance (or pos-
sibly because the schedule called for it) the Sun Kings were playing the Austins that night.
MR. TANNER GAVE a superlative performance. His is not the Me’hod School of acting; instead he exercises a subtle. restraint surprising in one so young.
For example, when Art Miranda carelessly stepped off first base in the third inning, and was tagged out, Mr. Tanner, standing in the third base coach’s box,
merely bowed his head and kicked the turf a few times. But he showed his controiled Passion in a subtle way — holding his breath until the back of his neck turned beet red and the powerful throat muscles stood out in bas relief. A fine dramatic performance.

Again, when the plate umpire hollered “STRIKE!” as a ball whizzed past the ankles of a Sun King batter, Mr. Tanner simply executed an about face, lifted his eyes to the heavens and said, “Oh, goodness gracious me!”
(He writes his own material, I understand.) 
In the fifth inning, and again in the sixth, the Austins began rattling the ball off or over the fences, with the result that our heroes were down by a score of 8 to 2 going into the bottom of the sixth. 
Mr. Tanner was not daunted. “Work hard!” he cried, sending his forces to the plate.
Work hard they did, scoring two runs in that inning, three in the seventh and two more in the eighth. The number of Kings getting on base gave Mr. Tanner a splendid opportunity to do his isometric exercises in the coach’s box, rubbing his
nose, scratching his head, brushing imaginary dirt off his uniform and dusting his hands. A lesser man would have been exhausted, but Mr. Tanner is obviously in fine condition, and was not even breathing hard by the ninth inning, at which time the score was knotted, 9 to 9.

At this point, Mr. Tanner summoned on stage one Frank Merriwell, better known as Gary Fischer. Mr. Fischer's entrance was quiet and unostentatious, but the Austins soon were wishing he had stayed in the dugout. For five innings he flung the
ball across the plate, giving up not a single hit. And in the 14th inning, catcher Pete Gongola having got on base with a walk, and then stealing second and going to third on a bad throw, Mr. Fischer strode to plate and rattled a ball off the left field fence to win the game.

FIRST TO GREET Mr. Fischer on his triumphant return to the dugout was our Mr. Tanner. “A splendid blow,” he said,
or words to that effect. Mr. Fischer bowed his head modestly, grinned sheepishly (he knows very well that pitchers aren’t supposed to hit) and muttered his thanks.
Whereupon the entire cast disappeared in the general direction of the showers.

Mr. Tanner undoubtedly deserves the “best actor” title given him by the Austins, but I think he should keep an eye on young Hank Cohen, son of the Sun King
coach Andy Cohen. Young Hank, who comes up to Mr. Tanner’s belt buckle and retrieves balls for the Sun Kings, spent his
idle moments during the game scooping up imaginary grounders and sighting in on imaginary fly balls. As far as I could see
he never missed a single imaginary catch.
It was a job of acting worthy of .. . of - - well, of a Chuck Tanner. He caught the real ones, too, by the way.


