BASEBALL AND CULTURE
It has been my observation down through the years that when the ladies
start out to do something they usually do it.
Right now, the Woman's Committee of El Paso Symphony Association is promoting a benefit baseball game between the
El Paso Sun Kings and Carlsbad to be played at Dudley Field at 8 p.m. June 12.
In that connection, Mrs. Bob Kolliner, chairman of the ticket sales committee,
brought me an interesting letter from Fresco Thompson, a big wheel in the Los
Angeles Dodgers, to Andy Cohen, a big wheel in the El Paso Sports Association, Inc. Here it is:
“Dear Andy:
“You really gave me quite a chore when you asked how cultural activities
can tie in with baseball. Strangely, the more I thought about it, the easier it became to consider professional baseball as part of the cultural activity of a community.
“Culture is defined in the dictionary as ‘development of the mind or body by education.’ Surely baseball can fall in this category.
“Baseball has, on occasion, been compared to the ballet. Anyone seeing Gil Hodges play first base, Willie Mays play centerfield or Luis Aparicio play shortstop must be intrigued by the sheer grace and rhythm of their movements. Ted Williams was a picture of fluid motion in his batting style. The grace and artistry of
Warren Spahn on the mouad must appeal to all who admire and appreciate technique and skill.
“The cultural activities of a community are generally confined to what is pleasing
to the ear such as opera or a musical show or what is appealing to the eye such as dancing or art exhibits.
“Any legitimate and wholesome activity that brings together the members of
a community solely for entertainment adds to the cultural development of that community, Certainly baseball which offers
the pleasing skills and finesse of the professional athlete should add to the cul-
tural activity of any community by giving its citizens a common meeting ground.”


