The High Beat by Clyde Huchet 
THE HIGH BEAT TOOK A stroll back through time last week and inadvertently ran
across a 41-year-old mystery involving local athletics.
The mystery concerns the missing Texas state basketball championship trophy of 1922.
To understand the background of the case it is necessary to retreat into time one year
more to the first Texas Interscholastic League state basketball championship tournament at Austin in 1921.
Coach Luther Coblentz’s El Paso High Tigers were invited to meet Fort Davis at Alpine
for the bi-district title and the right to advance to the state tournament.

Coblentz’s crew smashed a previously undefeated Fort Davis five, 30-10, to get into the tourney.
Right guard Andy Cohen scored five goals for the Tigers and received ovations for some
spectacular one-handed shots that were unheard of at the time.
Cohen, the spark plug of the squad, was one of the highest scorers in the state.
EL PASO’S RIGHT GUARD 
had Il field goals and a foul shot in the opening game of the tournament as the Tigers
stamped Shiner, 45-5. Sandi Esquivel had five field goals in the second half to also make the double figures.
Cohen totaled 13 in the semi-finals and Esquivel added six as the Tigers swept by Houston Central, 24-14, to make the fi-
nals against San Antonio Brackenridge.
El Paso High, with Esquivel getting 12 and Cohen eight, swept by Breckenridge, 25-11, for the state title. Cohen and
Louis Springer made the All-State first team and Esquivel made the second squad. Cohen
was awarded a trophy for outstanding sportsmanship.

THEN CAME THE 1922 tourney.
El Paso High started like a house-afire. Coach Coblentz’s
five rolled up one of the biggest scores of the time in the bi-district contest with a 100-4 decision over Pecos. They set six
records.
In the most lopsided game ever played by two possible state champions the Tigers surrendered one field goal and two
foul shots. Cohen connected for 23 field goals.
In the state tourney the Tigers had no trouble making
the finals with a 29-13 win over Kirbyville in the opener. The second opponent, Stephenville, gave the El Pasoans some trouble but fell 17-15.

The Tigers opened up in the semi-final game and won 20-13. Their opponent in the finals,
Lindale, swamped Texarkana 42-5 in its semi-final battle.
Lindale won its 30th of the season that night with a 27-15 win over the Tiger five for the
state title. Cohen once again made All-State and was joined by Stanley Dawson on the first
team. Carlos Lozano and H. H. Brown represented the Tigers
on the second five.

