Speaking of Sports Robert Phippa 
El Paso Proof Community Push Vital in the Minors
Roughly 97 per cent of the published stories dealing with the status of minor league ball these
days sound like they're from information gathered at the same wailing wall.
A pleasant exception is the report on El Paso, Tex. This team, playing in what is called a sophomore league, is feeling no pain.
The Associated Press checked and found that the team has been averaging 16 hundred paying customers. It had 13 thousand bucks in the bank and the account was growing. It drew 4,568 for opening night, a crowd that wouldn't be spurned in the American Association.
* * *
Solid
AND DON’T holler that shopworn doctrine about the winning pet El Paso has been hanging around third place all season.
The answers are various. The ball team started with a solid foundation when 40 civic leaders formed a non-profit association.
Each pitched in five hundred dollars in the form of a promisory note—to be paid if needed. But the 20-thousand-dollar financing has never been touched.
No Salary
IT IS interesting to note that Andy Cohen, who managed Denver in both the Western League
and the American Association, is one of the prime movers. Andy and three others operate the team without pay.
Three employes receive some salary on a part-time basis. There’s a working agreement with the Giants, Cohen’s original team, which is more than fair to El Paso.
The Giants supply all the players, pay the salary tab of over 36 hundred dollars a month, pay the manager, pay transportation costs of players to and from El Paso, and foot the bill for spring
training. The Giants do not share in the gate receipts.
‘Hungry’
ASKED WHY all the enthusiasm has jelled into admission money, El Paso observers cited four main reasons:
1. Local ownership and management on a _ non-profit basis, with profits earmarked for park improvements.
2. El Paso was hungry for baseball after a 3 ½  year absence.
3. The players are young and full of spirit.
4. Low admission prices. General admission costs one buck, a box seat $1.55.

