TWO GOOD PIECES OF LEGISLATION came out of the ee Athletic Conference presidents’ meeting this week at Denver. 
The WAC decided to move headquarters to Phoenix from Denver, and the conference joined the national letter-of-intent.
Bringing WAC headquarters to Phoenix fortifies Arizona’s role in the league and gets Commissioner Paul Brechler and
his staff back into the mainstream. Colorado has no WAC member. The only reason the conference stayed three years
in Denver was concession to leases and personal ties.
Now Phoenix will be focal point for conference releases’ and Arizona gains stature as meeting place and possible site for 
future WAC promotions such as all-star football or basketball games.
The letter-of-intent decision was long overdue. It gets the WAC in line with all major conferences except those Pacific
Coast schools leagued in the AAWU.
Coast Schools Still Can Lure Area Aces 
Up to now WAC schools couldn’t raid each other’s recruits —but were fair game for every other school in the nation.
Now all they have to worry about are those prunepickers to the West who still will be able to lure away athletes supposedly headed to WAC squads.
